// Cs4.grammar — C# 4.0 `dynamic` (predefined type) (T3.3.1).
//
// `dynamic` is a CONTEXTUAL KEYWORD in Roslyn: a plain identifier (IdentifierToken) in C# 1.0–3.0,
// a keyword in C# 4.0+. It is the SAME version-purity challenge as `var` (T3.1.2): a name that is a
// valid TypeName at an older version but a keyword at a newer one.
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to an ERROR. So a re-declared Cs4 alternative is reachable only when it
// is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs1 one.
//
// Tie-avoidance (the `dynamic` version-purity):
//   Cs1: PredefinedType = the 16 predefined types (Cs1:515-531); TypeName = !ReservedKeyword
//   Identifier (Cs1:23); Type = PredefinedType | QualifiedName | ... (Cs1:508-512); QualifiedName =
//   TypeName NamespaceSegment* (Cs1:21).
//   * v3 (Cs1+Cs2+Cs3): `dynamic` is NOT reserved (absent from Cs1 ReservedKeyword, not appended in
//     Cs2/Cs3) -> a valid TypeName -> `dynamic x = 5;` parses as a type-NAME declaration (type
//     `dynamic`), exactly like `var x = 5;` at v1. `dynamic` is NOT a PredefinedType at v3.
//   * v4 (Cs1+Cs2+Cs3+Cs4): `dynamic` IS reserved (appended below) -> NOT a TypeName -> the
//     QualifiedName route of `Type` fails; `dynamic` IS a PredefinedType (appended below) -> the
//     PredefinedType route matches. Only ONE `Type` alternative matches `dynamic` -> NO equal-length
//     tie.
//   Because `dynamic` is a full `Type` at v4, it flows through EVERY type position (field, property,
//   method return type, method parameter, local variable, ...) via the existing Cs1/Cs3 rules — no
//   per-position re-declaration is needed. This is the key difference from `var`: `var` is NOT a type
//   (it is only a local-declaration marker), so T3.1.2 needed a dedicated `LocalVariableDeclaration =
//   "var" ...` alternative. `dynamic` needs no such alternative — the Cs1 `Type`-based
//   LocalVariableDeclaration already handles `dynamic y = x;` at v4.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main):
//   * `dynamic` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword (IdentifierToken) — SyntaxKindExtensions.cs:48 ("Note that
//     'dynamic' is a contextual keyword, so it should never show up here."). Not a hard reserved
//     keyword -> a plain identifier in C# 1.0–3.0.
//   * At the SYNTAX level Roslyn parses `dynamic` as an IdentifierName (a type name), NOT a
//     PredefinedType: IsPredefinedType (SyntaxKindFacts.cs:316-340) lists the 16 predefined types and
//     does NOT include dynamic. The built-in dynamic type is a BINDER concept (DynamicTypeSymbol.cs;
//     Binder_Symbols.cs:897 resolves an IdentifierName "dynamic" to the built-in dynamic type when no
//     user type by that name exists) — a parser-vs-binder split.
//   * Our grammar models the version-purity at the PARSE level (dynamic is a PredefinedType at v4, a
//     TypeName at v3) for consistency with the established `var` (T3.1.2) and `partial` (T3.1.3)
//     precedent — a deliberate, documented deviation from Roslyn's pure binder-level handling.
//
// CS4-only: expression-position `dynamic` (member access / invocation / indexer on a dynamic VALUE)
// is a BINDER concern — its syntax is a plain IdentifierName + postfix operators, already parsed by
// Cs1, so no grammar change is needed here.

// === `dynamic` reservation (version-purity). Re-declares Cs1 ReservedKeyword; APPENDS "dynamic". ===
// `dynamic` is a plain identifier in C# 1.0–3.0 (a valid TypeName) but a keyword in C# 4.0+, so it is
// NOT added to Cs1 ReservedKeyword (that would break C# 1.0–3.0, where `dynamic` is a valid type/
// variable name). Appending here reserves `dynamic` only at version 4+. Consequence: at v4 `dynamic`
// is NOT a TypeName (`!ReservedKeyword Identifier` fails), so the QualifiedName route of `Type` fails
// and only the PredefinedType route (below) matches — no equal-length tie.
ReservedKeyword =
    | "dynamic";

// === `dynamic` predefined type (C# 4.0). Re-declares Cs1 PredefinedType; APPENDS "dynamic". ===
// At v4 `dynamic` is a valid `Type` via this alternative. It is mutually exclusive with the Cs1
// QualifiedName route at v4 (dynamic is reserved -> not a TypeName), so no tie. Being a full Type, it
// is usable in every type position (field/property/parameter/return type/local) via the existing
// Cs1/Cs3 rules.
PredefinedType =
    | "dynamic";

// ============================================================================
// T3.3.2 — C# 4.0 named arguments.
//
// A named argument is `Identifier ":" Expression` (the parameter name, a `:`, and the argument
// value). A positional argument is just `Expression`. In an argument list, the `:` after an
// identifier is unambiguously a named-argument separator (Roslyn ParseArgumentExpression,
// LanguageParser.cs:12583: CurrentToken is IdentifierToken && PeekToken(1) is ColonToken ->
// NameColon(Identifier, ColonToken)). The `:` is NOT a TDOPP binary operator (the only `:` in
// Expression is inside the conditional `Expression "?" Expression ":" Expression`, which requires
// a leading `?`), so `x: 5` is NOT an Expression — it requires the NamedArgument rule.
//
// The method-call argument list is `Invocation`, a NAMED ALTERNATIVE of `PostfixOp`
// (Cs1.grammar:725): `"(" (Expression; ",")* ")"`. A named alternative is inlined into its parent
// rule (it is NOT a separate RuleSymbol — Scope.AddSymbol registers only top-level rule
// statements), so `Invocation` CANNOT be re-declared directly; the parent `PostfixOp` must be
// re-declared to add a new alternative.
//
// Approach (T0.3 append-merge of `PostfixOp`):
//   * New `Argument` rule: `| NamedArgument = Identifier ":" Expression | Expression`. Within
//     `Argument`, longest-match disambiguates: `x: 5` -> NamedArgument (longer than `x`); `x`
//     (no `:`) -> Expression (NamedArgument fails: no `:`); `5` -> Expression (NamedArgument fails:
//     not an Identifier). No equal-length tie within `Argument`.
//   * Re-declare `PostfixOp` to APPEND `NamedInvocation = "(" (Argument; ",")* ")"`. For a
//     positional-only list `(x)`, BOTH the Cs1 `Invocation` and the Cs4 `NamedInvocation` match at
//     the same length; the engine keeps the FIRST (declaration-order) success (Parser.cs:296-316),
//     so the Cs1 `Invocation` wins (no error). For a list with a named argument `(x: 5)`, the Cs1
//     `Invocation` FAILS (`x: 5` is not an Expression), so the Cs4 `NamedInvocation` is the only
//     match. The Cs4 alternative is therefore reachable only for named-argument lists.
//
// Version purity: at v3 (Cs1+Cs2+Cs3, no Cs4) the `NamedInvocation` alternative is absent, so
// `N(x: 5)` REJECTS (the Cs1 `Invocation` fails: `x: 5` is not an Expression). At v4 the
// `NamedInvocation` is present, so `N(x: 5)` parses. (Roslyn: NamedArgumentBeforeCSharp4,
// ParserErrorMessageTests.cs:6140 — CS8024 "Feature 'named argument' is not available in C# 3".)
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   ParseArgumentExpression (12581): nameColon = (Identifier && Peek(1)==Colon) ? NameColon(...) :
//   null; expression = ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Expression); Argument(nameColon, refKind, expr).
//   ParseArgumentList (12475): `(` (ParseArgumentExpression,)* `)` (allowTrailingSeparator: false,
//   requireOneElement: false, so `()` is allowed and a trailing `,` is rejected).
//   The ORDER (positional before named) is a BINDER concern (CS1737); the PARSER accepts any order.
//   Syntax tests: ExpressionParsingTests.cs:1024 (TestCallWithNamedArgument, `a(B: b)`), :1186
//   (TestNewWithNamedArgument), :2280 (TestTupleWithTwoNamedArguments).
//
// CS4 only: the `ref`/`out` named arguments are a binder concern — the syntax is the same
// (`Identifier ":" Expression`), so no separate rule is needed.

// === `Argument`: one argument in a named-argument-capable list. Named union (the meta-grammar
// forbids `|` inside a group). Longest-match: `x: 5` -> NamedArgument (longer); `x` (no `:`) ->
// Expression; `5` -> Expression.
//
// T3.3.2 fix — the value/positional expression is `Expression : Comma`, NOT a plain `Expression`.
// A plain `Expression` is a TDOPP rule with minPrecedence 0, and the `Comma` operator (binding power
// 1, the lowest in the Cs1 precedence list) is applicable at minPrecedence 0 (Parser.cs:360:
// `precedence > minPrecedence || (== && right)` -> `1 > 0`). So a plain `Expression` greedily ABSORBS
// a following `,`: in `N(1, y: 6)` the first argument's `Expression` parses `1, y` (the Comma RHS is
// just `y`, since `y: 6` is not an Expression) and the argument list then fails on the leftover `: 6`
// -> the whole parse fails. `Expression : Comma` (a ReqRef with Precedence = Comma's binding power 1,
// RuleGenerator.cs:90-94) raises minPrecedence to 1, so the Comma postfix is NOT applicable
// (`1 > 1` is false) while every other operator (binding power >= 2) still is. The argument therefore
// stops at the `,` separator. This is the same minPrecedence mechanism Cs1 already uses
// (`Expression : Relational` in TypeIs/TypeAs, `Expression : Cast` in CastExpr) — no engine change.
// ===
Argument =
    | NamedArgument      = Identifier ":" Expression : Comma
    | PositionalArgument = Expression : Comma;

// === Named-argument invocation (C# 4.0). Re-declares `PostfixOp` (append, T0.3 merge). The Cs4
// alternative is a `(` argument list of `Argument`s. For a positional-only list the Cs1
// `Invocation` (declaration-order first) wins the equal-length match; for a list with a named
// argument the Cs1 `Invocation` fails and this is the only match. Roslyn ParseArgumentExpression
// (LanguageParser.cs:12581). ===
PostfixOp =
    | NamedInvocation = "(" (Argument; ",")* ")";
